    "narrative": "The LawDog lineup faces a challenging slate today, with **Geraldo Perdomo** emerging as our highest-rated start at 56.04. **Geraldo Perdomo** benefits from a favorable matchup against Phillips (xERA 4.33), providing a reliable floor despite a lackluster park factor. While **Taylor Ward** carries a slightly lower score of 52.71, he remains a cornerstone of the day's strategy; his projection is bolstered significantly by a +16% park adjustment and the premium afforded to his presumed lead-off position. Conversely, **Jeremiah Jackson** finds himself at the bottom of the startable tier at 47.81, where the brutal combination of Bryan Woo\u2019s high-end skill (-14% impact) and a projected #9 spot heavily weighs down his upside.\n\nStrategic depth remains thin as we navigate injuries and scheduling gaps. We are currently missing the production of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Lawrence Butler** due to team off-days, while our catching corps, anchored by **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz**, remains sidelined on the IL. This lack of rotation depth made the decision to play **Masyn Winn** (50.76) relatively straightforward, as he occupies the final active slot despite a neutral matchup against Scott. The margin between our active players and the bench is razor-thin today; **Gleyber Torres** (48.51) barely held his position over the alternative pool, saved largely by an 8% boost for his high-leverage #2 spot in the order. \n\nRisk management is the primary theme for today's roster construction. Both **Taylor Ward** and **Jeremiah Jackson** are subject to \"Lineup Pending\" status against Bryan Woo; should either shift lower in the batting order or get a surprise rest day, their efficiency scores will collapse instantly. While we prioritize speed and stolen base potential in this 5x5 format, today\u2019s board offers little in the way of elite sprint-speed upside, forcing us to rely on pure efficiency metrics. Managers should monitor the official lineup cards closely, as any deviation from our assumed batting orders could necessitate a late-game pivot for the LawDog lineup.",
